Title: THE WATER TANK — SIT-DOWN
Shoot: SAMPLE · sit-down interview · Cari asks, Jason answers · one setup, camera does not move
Shoot-Type: B
Interviewer: Cari

[[SAMPLE CONTENT. Made up for Cari to judge the FORMAT, not the shoot. The real questions
would come off the week's shot list.]]

EXT. BESIDE THE FINISHED TANK, IN THE SHADE — DAY

Jason sits on the edge of the tank pad. Cari sits just off the left of the lens; he looks at her, not at the camera.

!ON CAMERA: his eyeline going to Cari and not to the lens, and the whole tank behind him.

INTERVIEWER
Why did you put the tank up the hill instead of down by the house?

JASON
(in his own words)
Height is the only free pump there is. Every foot of hill is another half pound of pressure at the tap.

INTERVIEWER
What went wrong the first time you tried to level that pad?

JASON
(in his own words)
The ground looked flat and was not. Water found the low corner before the tank was even full.

= Cari and Jason up on the pad in the rain, working out whether the tarp went over the sand or under it.

INTERVIEWER
That afternoon in the rain when we were both stood on the pad working out where the tarp went — tell me where you were coming from on that.

JASON
(in his own words)
I wanted the sand dry more than I wanted to be done. That was the whole argument.

INTERVIEWER
If somebody watching has one weekend and no machine, what would you tell them to do first?

JASON
(in his own words)
Dig the high side down. Never build the low side up.

EXT. AT THE OUTLET FITTING — DAY

Jason crouches at the outlet and puts his hand on the fitting while he talks.

!ON CAMERA: his hand on the fitting, and the hose run going downhill out of frame.

INTERVIEWER
What is the one part of this that you would not cheap out on again?

JASON
(in his own words)
The fitting that goes through the wall of the tank. Everything else can be fixed with a shovel.
